<p>hewwo</p>
<p><strong>Initial thoughts:</strong> I like the idea. Kind the boiler room or dev room of the universe. It's a neat idea and there's a lot of room to explore and for the Foundation to start to uncover the laws and mechanisms of magic.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">more&nbsp;thoughts</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">less&nbsp;thoughts</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I do, however, have some issues with the concept. The biggest thing that sticks out to me is security. While it would make sense that it would be guarded against people who would inadvertently mess with the wrong machine and destroy the universe, robots or something constantly being alert to murder anyone who enters feels very cliche and inelegant. As you said, this is not an easy place to reach. If I were the Architect of the Universe and left behind a room of glorious engines, I would expect the people who found it to be powerful sages or natural explorers rather than raiders. To me, it would feel more natural if the security was a guide who was peaceful, helpful, and explained things up until the point that an engine was destroyed or tampered with.</p>
<p>However, if you were to take an eldritch slant, you could make it so understand these machines is like a fish trying to understand its aquarium filter. Then deadly security forces would be understandable.</p>
<p>Beyond that, it feels like a very dry story. Like cybersqyd said, it's kind of a passive, foundation poking around kind of deal. Like they really can't touch the machines or it could collapse the sun so why are they there?</p>
<p>If you can give me a good answer as to what the story would be, I'll give you my greenlight.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

